      "role": "user",
      "content": "The draft looks okay so far, but it's a bit thin. You were cut off at the measurement precision part—can you finish that second point and then expand the review? \n\nI need more depth on why that 3:1 SNR is a problem for the validity of their coherence claims. Also, try to make the overall structure look more robust. I'm looking for a professional, comprehensive review that doesn't leave any stone unturned in the methodology section.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "**Reviewer Comments Continued:**\n\n2. **Measurement Precision:**\n   A signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of 3:1, while typical for many non-laboratory EPR spectrometers, operates under the assumption that noise is white and uncorrelated—a condition that may not apply in the precision-driven context of observing quantum coherence. Their methodology briefly mentions adaptations to reduce 1/f noise which is paramount for materials exhibiting quantum coherence at room temperatures. For such a pioneering claim, a more detailed account of the pre-processing techniques to enhance signal purity, as well as the application of noise reduction strategies such as Digital Fourier Transform Spectroscopy (DFTS) or frequency mixing, seems to be lacking. Without a clear, quantitative analysis of the SNR, including mitigation steps taken to ensure its adequacy for detecting coherence, the reliability of their findings remains questionable.\n\n3. **Theoretical Foundation and Comparisons:**\n   The paper's claim lacks a thorough theoretical foundation for the expected SNR, given the unique conditions necessary for sustained coherence amidst disorder. They do not engage with existing literature on material-specific coherence lifetimes, decoherence mechanisms, nor how these benchmarks compare against the obtained SNR. Further, there is no comparative analysis",
      "source": "target",
